%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b8cb648fdf2cd024d97f20a78acfcd4c, type: 3}
  m_Name: PracticalGuide_Pendulum_Azm
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::VirtualLab.Azm.Core.PracticalGuide_Azm
  title: 'Simple Pendulum: find g'
  aim: "Find the acceleration due to gravity g by timing a simple pendulum at different
    lengths and plotting T\xB2 against L."
  description: A simple pendulum is a small bob hanging from a fixed point on a light,
    inextensible thread. Pulled aside slightly and released, it swings back and forth
    under gravity. You measure how long one complete swing (the time period T) takes,
    and how that changes with the length.
  hypothesis: "The time period depends on the length: T\xB2 is directly proportional
    to L, and does not depend on the bob's mass or the size of a small swing. So
    T\xB2 against L is a straight line through the origin."
  apparatus:
  - Retort stand with clamp and split cork
  - "Light, inextensible thread (about 100\u2013150 cm)"
  - Metal spherical bob
  - Metre scale
  - Vernier calipers
  - Stopwatch
  whatYouMeasure: "The bob's diameter d (vernier calipers) \u2192 r = d/2. For at
    least 5 lengths \u2113: the time t for 20 oscillations, twice, and the mean.
    Then L = \u2113 + r, T = t/20 and T\xB2. From the slope of T\xB2 against L: g
    = 4\u03C0\xB2 / slope."
  procedure:
  - Clamp the thread at the pivot; let the bob hang freely.
  - Measure the bob's diameter with vernier calipers; halve it to get r.
  - "Set a length \u2113, from the pivot to the top of the bob."
  - "Effective length: L = \u2113 + r."
  - "Pull the bob aside by a small angle (\u2264 15\xB0) and release from rest -
    no push."
  - Start the stopwatch as the bob passes the mean (centre) position.
  - Count 20 full oscillations; stop the stopwatch as it passes the centre again.
  - Record this time as t.
  - Time it once more at the same length; average the two times.
  - T = t / 20.
  - Change the length and repeat, for at least 5 different lengths.
  - "Tabulate \u2113, r, L, t, T and T\xB2 for every length."
  - "Plot T\xB2 (y-axis) against L (x-axis)."
  - "A straight line through the origin confirms that T\xB2 is proportional to L."
  - "g = 4\u03C0\xB2 / slope."
  formulas:
  - 'Time period: T = t / n  (n = 20)'
  - "Effective length: L = \u2113 + r"
  - "T\xB2 = (4\u03C0\xB2/g) L, so T\xB2 is proportional to L"
  - "g = 4\u03C0\xB2 / slope  (slope = T\xB2 / L)"
  precautions:
  - text: "Keep the angle small (\u2264 15\xB0)."
    why: Beyond that the pendulum no longer behaves like simple harmonic motion.
  - text: "Use the effective length L = \u2113 + r."
    why: The bob swings about its centre; the radius matters, especially for short
      pendulums.
  - text: Time 20 oscillations, never one.
    why: It spreads your reaction-time error over many swings.
  - text: Start and stop at the mean position.
    why: The bob moves fastest there, so the moment is easiest to judge.
  - text: Release from rest - no push.
    why: A push adds energy and invalidates the simple-pendulum assumption.
  - text: Avoid draughts.
    why: Air currents make the bob swing in an ellipse instead of a flat plane.
  - text: Use a light, inextensible thread.
    why: A stretchy or heavy thread changes the effective length during the swing.
  - text: Keep the same person timing.
    why: Any reaction-time bias stays the same for every trial.
  - text: Read the scale at eye level.
    why: Avoids parallax error in the length.
  observationTable: "Obs. No. | \u2113 (cm) | r (cm) | L = \u2113 + r (cm) | n |
    t\u2081 (s) | t\u2082 (s) | mean t (s) | T = t/n (s) | T\xB2 (s\xB2) - with n
    = 20 for every row and at least 5 rows."
